# Target C-II — fixed-angle antipodal dipoles force bilateral diameter loading of their birth layers

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` topological/metric strengthening; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** any fixed `rho>0` wide exact-antipodal family on one side of the common hub inside the ordered O--O path  
**Dependencies:** phase-free path-region switching identity PRS.4; opposite-terminal O--O path topology; fixed-angle central-diamond endpoint separation; full-source perimeter bound `P(K)<=pi D`; diameter endpoints are convex-hull vertices  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_wide_dipole_bilateral_birth_loading_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The fixed-angle theorem gives two switching diameter chords at every `rho`-wide exact-antipodal interface.  The central-diamond theorem shows that **any endpoint of one chord is macroscopically separated from any endpoint of the other**.  Because both chords are born at the same path layer, this metric separation interacts with the exact two-arc topology of an O--O path region.

The result is an almost-exact bilateral loading theorem.

Let

\[
R_0\xleftrightarrow{\tau_1}R_1\xleftrightarrow{\tau_2}\cdots\xleftrightarrow{\tau_m}R_m
\tag{BL.1}
\]

be the selected path, with all `tau_i` joining the same two disjoint outer-boundary host arcs.  For an internal layer `R_i`, let

\[
C_i^+,C_i^-
\tag{BL.2}
\]

be the two outer-boundary subarcs cut out between the endpoints of `tau_i` and `tau_{i+1}` on the two host arcs.

Fix `rho>0`.  For every `rho`-wide exact-antipodal interface `i`, choose its two fixed-angle blocker diameter edges `e_i^+,e_i^-`.  By the switching identity, each has exactly one endpoint in `R_i`; call these birth endpoints

\[
x_i^+,x_i^-\in R_i.
\tag{BL.3}
\]

Then the number of wide interfaces for which `x_i^+` and `x_i^-` lie on the **same** host subarc is bounded independently of `D`:

\[
\boxed{
N_{\rm same}(D,\rho)
\le
{\pi D\over D\sin(\rho/2)-2L_D},
}
\tag{BL.4}
\]

where

\[
L_D=\sqrt{D\,3^{1/4}\sqrt D+\sqrt3 D}
=O(D^{3/4}).
\tag{BL.5}
\]

Consequently

\[
\boxed{
\limsup_{D\to\infty}N_{\rm same}(D,\rho)
\le{\pi\over\sin(\rho/2)}<\infty.
}
\tag{BL.6}
\]

Thus all but `O_rho(1)` wide exact-antipodal interfaces are **bilateral**:

\[
\boxed{
\{x_i^+,x_i^-\}
\text{ meets both }C_i^+\text{ and }C_i^-.
}
\tag{BL.7}
\]

If the wide-EA side has positive linear density, this produces two linearly long ordered sequences of distinct actual diameter endpoints, one on each host arc, indexed by the same birth-interface set.

This eliminates a remaining local regression in which both fixed-angle blockers at each interface could hide on one boundary side of the strip.

---

## 1. A switching blocker has one birth endpoint in the physical layer

For the exact O--O path coordinates, PRS.4 identifies the switching set at internal layer `R_i` as

\[
H_{sw,i}
=\{xy\in H:\rho(x)=R_i\text{ or }\rho(y)=R_i\}.
\tag{BL.8}
\]

On the selected side of the common diameter-routing hub all switching is one-sided toward the hub.  Hence every switching diameter edge at interface `i` has exactly one endpoint in `R_i` and its other endpoint in a hubward region.

Therefore each chosen blocker edge `e_i^\pm` has a well-defined birth endpoint `x_i^\pm` in `R_i`.

Every endpoint of an actual diameter edge is an exposed, hence extreme, point of the full convex hull `K`.  The only hull pieces belonging to the region between two consecutive proper crosscuts `tau_i,tau_{i+1}` are the two boundary subarcs `C_i^+,C_i^-`.  Thus

\[
\boxed{x_i^\pm\in C_i^+\cup C_i^-.}
\tag{BL.9}
\]

The interiors of the `C_i^+` are pairwise disjoint as `i` varies, and the same is true for the `C_i^-`; altogether these subarcs are occurrence-disjoint pieces of the outer boundary except for harmless shared endpoints.

---

## 2. Wide dipoles separate their two birth endpoints by a linear distance

The wide-dipole central-diamond theorem proves that if the projective angle of the two diameter chords is at least `rho`, then every endpoint of one chord and every endpoint of the other satisfy

\[
|p-q|
\ge D\sin{\rho\over2}-2L_D.
\tag{BL.10}
\]

Apply this to the two birth endpoints:

\[
\boxed{
|x_i^+-x_i^-|
\ge D\sin{\rho\over2}-2L_D.
}
\tag{BL.11}
\]

For fixed `rho>0` the right side is positive and asymptotic to `D sin(rho/2)`.

No KKT multiplier or local train-length estimate enters this metric statement.

---

## 3. Same-side birth consumes a macroscopic disjoint hull arc

Suppose both birth endpoints of a wide interface lie on the same host subarc, say

\[
x_i^+,x_i^-\in C_i^+.
\]

The polygonal arc length of `C_i^+` between these two vertices is at least their Euclidean distance.  Hence (BL.11) gives

\[
\operatorname{len}(C_i^+)
\ge D\sin{\rho\over2}-2L_D.
\tag{BL.12}
\]

The same statement holds with `+` replaced by `-`.

For different layer indices, the corresponding host subarcs have disjoint interiors.  Therefore, if `N_same` wide interfaces have same-side birth pairs, summing (BL.12) over the particular subarc used by each interface yields

\[
N_{\rm same}
\left(D\sin{\rho\over2}-2L_D\right)
\le P(K).
\tag{BL.13}
\]

Cauchy's perimeter-diameter inequality gives

\[
P(K)\le\pi D.
\tag{BL.14}
\]

Equations (BL.13)--(BL.14) prove (BL.4).  Divide by `D` and use `L_D/D->0` to obtain (BL.6).

Thus a fixed-angle pair can use one host side twice only a bounded number of times over the entire path, regardless of the number of selected layers.

---

## 4. Linear wide-EA density becomes bilateral linear endpoint density

Let `W_D(rho)` be any `rho`-wide exact-antipodal interface family with

\[
|W_D(\rho)|\ge\lambda D-o(D)
\tag{BL.15}
\]

for fixed `lambda>0`.  By (BL.6), after deleting only `O_rho(1)` interfaces, every remaining interface has one birth endpoint on each host subarc:

\[
x_i^{(+)}\in C_i^+,
\qquad
x_i^{(-)}\in C_i^-.
\tag{BL.16}
\]

Regions are disjoint, so all these birth endpoints are distinct across different `i`.  Therefore each of the two global host arcs contains at least

\[
\boxed{
\lambda D-o(D)
}
\tag{BL.17}
\]

distinct actual diameter endpoints, one in each of a linearly large ordered family of layer subarcs.

Moreover the order is inherited directly from the proper-crosscut path:

```text
host arc + :  ... C_i^+ ... C_j^+ ...
host arc - :  ... C_i^- ... C_j^- ...
```

with the corresponding reversal determined by the orientation of the two host arcs.  The same interface index labels one endpoint on each side.

In the winning side of the parameterized robust fork one may take

\[
\lambda={c_0-6\rho\over2}
\tag{BL.18}
\]

up to the usual `o(1)` loss whenever the wide-EA alternative is the larger side.

---

## 5. Frontier effect

The exact-antipodal branch has now acquired three simultaneous scales:

```text
local tag:
    both blockers are born at the same path layer R_i;

central scale:
    their four-endpoint hull contains one common radius-Theta_rho(D) central disk;
    their diagonal crossing lies O_rho(D^(3/4)) from the common circumcenter;

outer-boundary scale:
    except at O_rho(1) interfaces, the two birth endpoints occupy opposite
    host-arc pieces C_i^+, C_i^- of that same layer.
```

Thus a linear wide-EA survivor is a **bilaterally loaded ordered strip family**: linearly many consecutive birth layers each carry one actual diameter endpoint on each outer side, while the two associated diameter diagonals cross centrally at fixed angle.

This is closer to the train rectangle than the earlier unstructured dipole statement.  A remaining closure argument can now compare the two ordered birth-endpoint sequences against the common transverse train family and the near-central antipodal partner map.  Merely placing both blockers on one side of each strip is no longer a linear-density escape.

No C-II or Level-I closure is claimed.

**Disposition:** proved that same-host-side birth of a fixed-angle antipodal blocker pair occurs only `O_rho(1)` times; any positive-density wide-EA branch is bilaterally diameter-loaded on the two ordered host arcs. C-II and Level I remain open.
